# A publicly-trusted Let's Encrypt certificate for a bare IP address.
#
# Generally available since 15 January 2026, and hedged about in ways that
# matter operationally:
#
#   · Only the `shortlived` profile issues for IP identifiers. Certificates
#     live 160 hours — under seven days — so renewal is not a background
#     detail: a few days of broken ACME and every device gets a hard error.
#   · Only http-01 and tls-alpn-01 validate an IP. dns-01 cannot, by
#     construction. Keep 80 and 443 both reachable.
#   · Five certificates per exact identifier set per 7 days, with no override
#     available. Iterate against the staging directory, never production —
#     set METACLAUDE_ACME_DIR to
#     https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory while testing.
#   · Private ranges are refused outright: RFC1918, CGNAT (100.64/10, which is
#     Tailscale), link-local. This mode needs a genuinely public address.
#
# `dir` is named explicitly rather than left to the default issuer pair,
# because that pair's ZeroSSL half cannot issue for IPs and would fail over to
# a CA that always refuses.
#
# `profile` is still experimental in Caddy and rests on an Internet-Draft, not
# an RFC — which is why compose.yml pins the Caddy version rather than
# following a tag.
tls {
	issuer acme {
		dir {$METACLAUDE_ACME_DIR:https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory}
		email {$METACLAUDE_TLS_EMAIL}
		profile shortlived
	}
}
